// GitHub issue #482: what does an IntrusivePool WALK actually tolerate?
//
// Mutating a pool requires the lock as a matter of typing -- insert,
// remove and reclaim all take `borrow IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id]`, and
// another pool's guard is a compile error. Walking one requires nothing:
// intrusive_pool_first_slot, intrusive_pool_next_slot and
// intrusive_pool_probe_slot take no guard at all. The issue asks whether
// that asymmetry is a hole, and asks for the measurement BEFORE the
// design, on the pattern issue #479 Group A established.
//
// WHAT THE RACE IS, PRECISELY
//
// Reading the file rather than assuming: an insert never rewrites the
// chunk chain a walk follows, and a remove usually does not either. The
// one operation that does is a chunk RELEASE, and there are two of them --
// intrusive_pool_reclaim, and intrusive_pool_remove itself when the chunk
// it emptied is past this pool's retained reserve. A release unlinks the
// chunk, scrubs every byte of it, and hands its pages back to the page
// allocator. So the hazard is exactly the issue's own words: "a walk
// following a pointer that has been rewritten" -- a walker that has
// already read `next_chunk` and holds an address into a chunk that is
// then released.
//
// HOW THIS PRODUCES IT: A RENDEZVOUS, NOT A RACE
//
// The first version of this file chased that window statistically --
// thousands of churn rounds, hoping the walker was inside one when a
// release landed. That was the wrong instrument twice over, and both
// failures are worth recording because they are the reason this file
// looks the way it does:
//
// - It was EXPENSIVE. Every round released a whole multi-page chunk,
//   which scrubs every byte of it, so one 2048-round pass cost 12.4
//   SECONDS of real boot time (measured: 772445438 ticks at 62.5 MHz).
//   At four times that it delayed the boot far enough to break this same
//   boot's unrelated network self-test -- `virtio net: icmp timeout`
//   where a healthy boot says `icmp echo reply ok`, and a 51-second
//   interactive-HTTPd stall behind it. A probe that breaks an unrelated
//   fixture to make its own point is not a probe this tree can keep.
// - It was UNRELIABLE where it mattered most. QEMU's round-robin vCPU
//   scheduling crossed the window readily; the RPi5 board did not cross
//   it in either of two boots, so the hazard went untested on the only
//   target whose concurrency is real.
//
// kernel/CONCURRENCY.md already states the rule this file should have
// followed from the start, and `df06fc8` had already applied it to the
// freelist and pid probes: rendezvous FIRST, retry only as the fallback.
// Split the two cores at the exact instruction boundary that matters and
// hold them there, rather than running both flat out and hoping.
//
// So the walker publishes the address the moment `next_slot` hands it one
// that belongs to the extra chunk, and WAITS. Core 0 keeps that chunk
// alive until the walker says it is holding it, releases it, and only
// then lets the walker proceed to probe. Every round reproduces the
// hazard by construction:
//
//     core 1: cursor = next_slot(...)   -- a legitimately obtained address
//             publish it, wait for the release
//             probe_slot(cursor)        -- MUST answer NoPayload
//
//     core 0: insert           -- the extra chunk exists
//             wait for the walker to be holding it
//             remove           -- unlink, scrub, pages back to the allocator
//             publish released, wait for the probe
//
// Three problems fall to that one change. The rounds drop from 2048 to
// POOL_WALK_ROUNDS because nothing is left to chance, taking the cost
// with them; the verdict stops depending on which machine the boot landed
// on; and the confound that made the statistical version ambiguous is
// gone too, since core 0 cannot reach the next round's allocation -- which
// could hand the same page straight back -- until the walker has already
// probed.
//
// WHAT IT ASSERTS
//
// - `refused`: probes of a released chunk's address that answered
//   NoPayload. Must equal the rounds that rendezvoused: this is the
//   property #482 asks about, and it holds because
//   intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid asks the PAGE ALLOCATOR who owns the
//   page (issue #384's page_owner_tag_at) before touching it.
// - `admitted`: probes that answered Live for a released chunk. Must be
//   ZERO. Nonzero is the defect this file exists to catch -- a walk
//   dereferencing a page the pool no longer owns.
// - `floor_bad`: a floor slot whose payload was not the sentinel. The
//   floor is written once at setup and never touched again, so this is a
//   deterministic control: it says the walk reads live slots correctly,
//   and it would catch the walk straying out of the chunk it is in.
//
// Current limitations:
// - IT DOES NOT COVER THE PAYLOAD POINTER'S LIFETIME. probe_slot's answer
//   is a snapshot; a caller holding the `*T` that payload_of hands back
//   across a concurrent remove is issue #488's shape, not this one, and
//   the eighteen declared `_unproven_` escapes are where that still lives.
// - IT TESTS RELEASE, NOT INSERT. Measured while building this and worth
//   recording, because the pool's own contract says it and nothing had
//   observed it from the outside: a lockless walker CAN see a slot's first
//   word holding the free-chain LINK that used to occupy it, in the window
//   after intrusive_pool_insert_unchecked sets `slot.generation` nonzero
//   and before the caller -- or insert_zeroed's own zero-loop -- writes the
//   payload. `intrusive_pool_insert`'s comment states the contract exactly
//   ("nothing zeroes the storage here... a freshly allocated payload is
//   uninitialized, write it before reading it"), and the observed values
//   were slot addresses inside the same chunk, which is what a free-chain
//   link is. That is a second, narrower hazard than this file's subject,
//   it belongs to insert rather than to walking, and it is recorded here
//   rather than re-measured every boot.
// - The verdict is a LOWER BOUND on a real machine, like every QEMU
//   concurrency result -- see kernel/CONCURRENCY.md.

use "kernel/lib/intrusive_pool.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb";

// Deterministic now, so this is "enough to show it repeatedly", not
// "enough for luck to strike". Each round releases a whole chunk, which
// scrubs every byte of it, so rounds are not free -- see the cost this
// file's header records for what 2048 of them bought.
const POOL_WALK_ROUNDS: usize = 64;
const POOL_WALK_SENTINEL: usize = 0x5AFE5107;

// A walk's own termination bound: enough slot-hops that a healthy chain
// (a floor chunk plus at most one extra) is covered several times over,
// so this never doubles as a source of truncation. A chain being rewritten
// underneath is the case this exists for, and a walk that trusted the
// chain to terminate would hang instead of reporting.
const POOL_WALK_STEP_BUDGET: usize = 64;

// A payload wide enough that a chunk holds only a FEW slots, so the floor
// below can FILL one chunk while carrying a bounded number of addresses to
// rebuild owners with at teardown. A `usize` payload gives ~255 slots per
// chunk and would need 255 of each.
struct PoolWalkItem {
    stamp: usize;
    filler: [usize; 250];
}

// The floor is one full chunk. Sized generously against a chunk geometry
// this file does not want to hard-code; the setup refuses rather than
// half-fills if the real number is bigger.
const POOL_WALK_FLOOR: usize = 16;

// Both cores wait for each other at the rendezvous. Bounded in TIME, not
// in iterations, because the two targets disagree about what an iteration
// costs -- kernel/CONCURRENCY.md's rule, and the one this file's first
// version broke by tuning a spin count that meant different things on
// QEMU and on the board.
const POOL_WALK_RENDEZVOUS_SHIFT: i64 = 2;

private let mut pool_walk_pool: IntrusivePool(PoolWalkItem);
private let mut pool_walk_ready: bool = false;
// The floor's (address, generation) pairs, kept so the owners can be
// rebuilt and removed at teardown -- the same arrangement
// kernel/kernel/pool_contention_evidence.tkb's own floor uses, and for the
// same reason: a linear owner cannot be parked in an array.
private let mut pool_walk_floor_address: [IntrusiveSlotAddress; POOL_WALK_FLOOR];
private let mut pool_walk_floor_generation: [usize; POOL_WALK_FLOOR];
private let mut pool_walk_floor_held: usize = 0;
// Where the floor chunk's slots start and stop. An address outside this
// range is the extra chunk's, which is how the walker knows it has
// crossed without needing this file to know the pool's private geometry.
private let mut pool_walk_floor_base: usize = 0;
private let mut pool_walk_floor_end: usize = 0;

private let mut pool_walk_armed: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_active: usize;
// The rendezvous, as a round number rather than a pair of flags: core 0
// publishes `seq`, the walker answers `held_seq` when it is holding that
// round's address, core 0 answers `released_seq` once the chunk is gone,
// and the walker answers `probed_seq` once it has asked. A sequence
// cannot be missed the way a flag can when the other core is late -- the
// lesson issue #510 left on every probe in this directory.
private let mut pool_walk_seq: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_held_seq: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_released_seq: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_probed_seq: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_stale: usize;

private let mut pool_walk_rounds_met: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_refused: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_admitted: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_floor_bad: usize;
private let mut pool_walk_steps: usize;

private fn pool_walk_armed_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_armed) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_active_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_active) as usize;
}

// GitHub issue #510: entries, counted and never cleared. This probe's
// secondary loops `while (armed == 1)` and so does NOT leave while armed,
// which makes `active` a stable level here and would have made it a safe
// gate. It is still not what the gate reads: "safe because of how the other
// core's loop happens to be written" is the kind of reasoning that stops
// being true when somebody edits that loop, and scripts/check_probe_entry_gates.py
// cannot see it. A count that only grows needs no such argument.
private let mut pool_walk_entries: usize;

private fn pool_walk_entries_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_entries) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_seq_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_seq) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_held_seq_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_held_seq) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_released_seq_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_released_seq) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_probed_seq_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_probed_seq) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_stale_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_stale) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_refused_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_refused) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_admitted_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_admitted) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_floor_bad_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_floor_bad) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_steps_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_steps) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_rounds_met_address() -> usize {
    return (&pool_walk_rounds_met) as usize;
}

private fn pool_walk_init() {
    if (pool_walk_ready) { return; }
    pool_walk_ready = true;
    // ZERO retained chunks, which is the whole setup: the chunk core 0
    // adds is released the moment it empties, rather than being held back
    // as a reserve.
    intrusive_pool_init_retaining(&pool_walk_pool, 0);
}

// Is this address one of the floor chunk's slots? The floor never moves
// and is never released, so anything outside it is the extra chunk core 0
// is creating and destroying.
private fn pool_walk_in_floor(address: usize) -> bool {
    return address >= pool_walk_floor_base && address < pool_walk_floor_end;
}

// -- The walking core --------------------------------------------------

fn pool_walk_contention_is_armed() -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_armed_address())
    } == 1;
}

// One enumeration. Floor slots are read and checked; the first address
// that is NOT the floor's is the extra chunk, and that is where this core
// stops and hands the round to core 0.
private fn pool_walk_once() !{unsafe} {
    let mut cursor: usize = intrusive_pool_first_slot(&pool_walk_pool);
    let mut budget: usize = POOL_WALK_STEP_BUDGET;
    let mut crossed: bool = false;
    while (cursor != 0 && budget != 0 && crossed == false) {
        budget = budget - 1;
        unsafe { atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(pool_walk_steps_address(), 1); }
        if (pool_walk_in_floor(cursor)) {
            match intrusive_pool_probe_slot(&pool_walk_pool, cursor) {
                IntrusiveSlotProbe::NoPayload => {}
                IntrusiveSlotProbe::Live(slot_view) => {
                    let payload: *PoolWalkItem =
                        intrusive_pool_payload_of(&pool_walk_pool, slot_view);
                    // The floor is written once at setup and never touched
                    // again, so this is a control rather than a race: a
                    // deviation means the walk read something that is not
                    // the slot it named.
                    if (payload.stamp != POOL_WALK_SENTINEL) {
                        unsafe {
                            atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(
                                pool_walk_floor_bad_address(), 1);
                        }
                    }
                    intrusive_view_release(slot_view);
                }
            }
            cursor = intrusive_pool_next_slot(&pool_walk_pool, cursor);
        } else {
            // THE RENDEZVOUS. This address came out of next_slot following
            // the floor chunk's `next_chunk`, which is exactly the pointer
            // issue #482 says a release rewrites underneath a walk. Publish
            // it and stop; core 0 will release the chunk it names and then
            // let this core ask about it.
            crossed = true;
            let round: usize = unsafe {
                atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_seq_address())
            };
            unsafe {
                atomic_store_release(pool_walk_stale_address(), cursor);
                atomic_store_release(pool_walk_held_seq_address(), round);
            }
            let window: i64 = read_cntfrq() >> POOL_WALK_RENDEZVOUS_SHIFT;
            let start: i64 = read_cntpct();
            let mut released: bool = false;
            while (released == false && read_cntpct() - start < window &&
                   unsafe {
                       atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_armed_address())
                   } == 1) {
                if (unsafe {
                        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_released_seq_address())
                    } >= round) {
                    released = true;
                }
            }
            if (released) {
                // The whole point, in one call: the chunk this address
                // names has been unlinked, scrubbed and handed back, and
                // the walk is asking about it anyway.
                match intrusive_pool_probe_slot(&pool_walk_pool, cursor) {
                    IntrusiveSlotProbe::NoPayload => {
                        unsafe {
                            atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(
                                pool_walk_refused_address(), 1);
                        }
                    }
                    IntrusiveSlotProbe::Live(slot_view) => {
                        unsafe {
                            atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(
                                pool_walk_admitted_address(), 1);
                        }
                        intrusive_view_release(slot_view);
                    }
                }
                unsafe {
                    atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(
                        pool_walk_rounds_met_address(), 1);
                }
            }
            unsafe {
                atomic_store_release(pool_walk_probed_seq_address(), round);
            }
        }
    }
}

fn pool_walk_contention_secondary_run() !{unsafe} {
    unsafe {
        atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(pool_walk_entries_address(), 1);
        atomic_store_release(pool_walk_active_address(), 1);
    }
    while (unsafe {
               atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_armed_address())
           } == 1) {
        pool_walk_once();
    }
    unsafe { atomic_store_release(pool_walk_active_address(), 0); }
}

// -- The churning core -------------------------------------------------

private fn pool_walk_report(stage: *u8) !{unsafe} {
    kernel_boot_log("pool walk stage: ");
    kernel_boot_log(stage);
    kernel_boot_log("\n");
    kernel_boot_log("pool walk counts: rounds=");
    uart_put_udec(POOL_WALK_ROUNDS);
    kernel_boot_log(" met=");
    uart_put_udec(unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_rounds_met_address())
    });
    kernel_boot_log(" refused=");
    uart_put_udec(unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_refused_address())
    });
    kernel_boot_log(" admitted=");
    uart_put_udec(unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_admitted_address())
    });
    kernel_boot_log(" floor_bad=");
    uart_put_udec(unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_floor_bad_address())
    });
    kernel_boot_log(" steps=");
    uart_put_udec(unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_steps_address())
    });
    kernel_boot_log(" per_chunk=");
    uart_put_udec(intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(&pool_walk_pool));
    kernel_boot_log("\n");
}

// Fill one chunk and keep it, so a walk always has something to enumerate
// and the chunk it starts in is never the one being released. The owners
// are discharged and the pairs kept, because a linear owner cannot live in
// an array; teardown rebuilds them.
private fn pool_walk_floor_take() -> bool !{allocates, unsafe} {
    let per_chunk: usize = intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(&pool_walk_pool);
    if (per_chunk > POOL_WALK_FLOOR) { return false; }
    for i: usize in 0..<POOL_WALK_FLOOR {
        if (i < per_chunk) {
            let guard = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool_walk_pool);
            let taken = intrusive_pool_insert_zeroed(&pool_walk_pool, guard);
            intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool_walk_pool, guard);
            match taken {
                IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory => { return false; }
                IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(owner) => {
                    intrusive_pool_ref(&pool_walk_pool, owner).stamp =
                        POOL_WALK_SENTINEL;
                    pool_walk_floor_address[i] = intrusive_owner_slot(owner);
                    pool_walk_floor_generation[i] =
                        intrusive_owner_generation(owner);
                    pool_walk_floor_held = pool_walk_floor_held + 1;
                    intrusive_owner_drop(owner);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    // Only the floor exists at this moment, so the chain's first slot is
    // its base, and its slots run one stride apart from there.
    pool_walk_floor_base = intrusive_pool_first_slot(&pool_walk_pool);
    pool_walk_floor_end = pool_walk_floor_base +
        per_chunk * intrusive_pool_slot_stride(&pool_walk_pool);
    return pool_walk_floor_base != 0;
}

private fn pool_walk_floor_give_back() -> bool !{unsafe} {
    let mut ok: bool = true;
    for i: usize in 0..<POOL_WALK_FLOOR {
        if (i < pool_walk_floor_held) {
            let owner = intrusive_owner_new(&pool_walk_pool,
                                            pool_walk_floor_address[i],
                                            pool_walk_floor_generation[i]);
            let guard = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool_walk_pool);
            if (intrusive_remove_succeeded(
                    intrusive_pool_remove(&pool_walk_pool, guard, owner))
                    == false) {
                ok = false;
            }
            intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool_walk_pool, guard);
        }
    }
    pool_walk_floor_held = 0;
    let reclaim_guard = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool_walk_pool);
    let released: usize =
        intrusive_pool_reclaim(&pool_walk_pool, reclaim_guard);
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool_walk_pool, reclaim_guard);
    return ok;
}

// Wait, bounded in time, for the other core to reach `target` on one of
// the rendezvous sequences. False means it did not, which is a give-up
// this file reports rather than a hang.
private fn pool_walk_await(sequence_address: usize, target: usize)
        -> bool !{unsafe} {
    let window: i64 = read_cntfrq() >> POOL_WALK_RENDEZVOUS_SHIFT;
    let start: i64 = read_cntpct();
    let mut met: bool = false;
    while (met == false && read_cntpct() - start < window) {
        if (unsafe { atomic_load_acquire(sequence_address) } >= target) {
            met = true;
        }
    }
    return met;
}

// One round: make the extra chunk, wait for the walker to be holding an
// address in it, release it, and wait for the walker to have asked.
// The four ways a round can fail are four different things, and the stage
// this returns is what the caller prints. Collapsing them into one word is
// the defect GitHub issue #510 was filed over -- "the lock leaked", "the
// race did not reproduce" and "one core never ran" all reading `failed` --
// and this file had it too until the end-of-session audit that also
// produced the numbers in its header.
private fn pool_walk_round(round: usize) -> *u8 !{allocates, unsafe} {
    unsafe { atomic_store_release(pool_walk_seq_address(), round); }
    let guard = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool_walk_pool);
    let taken = intrusive_pool_insert_zeroed(&pool_walk_pool, guard);
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool_walk_pool, guard);
    match taken {
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory => {
            return "allocator-empty" as *u8;
        }
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(owner) => {
            intrusive_pool_ref(&pool_walk_pool, owner).stamp =
                POOL_WALK_SENTINEL;
            // The walker has to have the address BEFORE the chunk goes,
            // or the round proves nothing. This wait is what replaces the
            // luck the first version of this file depended on.
            let held: bool =
                pool_walk_await(pool_walk_held_seq_address(), round);
            let release = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool_walk_pool);
            let removed: bool = intrusive_remove_succeeded(
                intrusive_pool_remove(&pool_walk_pool, release, owner));
            intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool_walk_pool, release);
            // Checked before `held`: a chunk this probe could not give back
            // is a leak, and saying "the walk never arrived" about it would
            // send the reader to the wrong core.
            if (removed == false) { return "extra-chunk-not-removed" as *u8; }
            if (held == false) { return "walk-never-held-it" as *u8; }
            unsafe {
                atomic_store_release(pool_walk_released_seq_address(), round);
            }
            // Do not start the next round -- whose allocation could be
            // handed the very page just released -- until the walker has
            // asked about the address it is holding.
            if (pool_walk_await(pool_walk_probed_seq_address(), round)
                    == false) {
                return "walk-never-probed-it" as *u8;
            }
            return 0 as *u8;
        }
    }
}

fn pool_walk_contention_probe() -> bool !{allocates, unsafe} {
    pool_walk_init();
    let live_before: usize = page_allocator_live_count();
    if (pool_walk_floor_take() == false) {
        pool_walk_report("floor-not-taken" as *u8);
        return false;
    }
    let entries_before: usize = unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_entries_address())
    };
    unsafe { atomic_store_release(pool_walk_armed_address(), 1); }

    let entry_window: i64 = read_cntfrq() >> 2;
    let entry_start: i64 = read_cntpct();
    let mut entered: bool = false;
    while (entered == false && read_cntpct() - entry_start < entry_window) {
        if (unsafe {
                atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_entries_address())
            } > entries_before) {
            entered = true;
        }
    }
    if (entered == false) {
        unsafe { atomic_store_release(pool_walk_armed_address(), 0); }
        pool_walk_report("secondary-never-entered" as *u8);
        return false;
    }

    let mut rounds_ok: bool = true;
    let mut round_stage: *u8 = 0 as *u8;
    for round: usize in 0..<POOL_WALK_ROUNDS {
        if (rounds_ok) {
            let stage: *u8 = pool_walk_round(round + 1);
            if (stage != (0 as *u8)) {
                rounds_ok = false;
                round_stage = stage;
            }
        }
    }
    unsafe { atomic_store_release(pool_walk_armed_address(), 0); }

    // Provably out before the accounting is read, for the reason the pool
    // probe next door states: a core still inside would make a leak of a
    // page it is holding.
    let settle_window: i64 = read_cntfrq() >> 2;
    let settle_start: i64 = read_cntpct();
    let mut settled: bool = false;
    while (settled == false && read_cntpct() - settle_start < settle_window) {
        if (unsafe {
                atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_active_address())
            } == 0) {
            settled = true;
        }
    }

    if (rounds_ok == false) {
        pool_walk_report(round_stage);
        return false;
    }
    if (settled == false) {
        pool_walk_report("secondary-never-left" as *u8);
        return false;
    }
    pool_walk_report("complete" as *u8);
    if (pool_walk_floor_give_back() == false) {
        kernel_boot_log("pool walk: the floor this probe held was not given back\n");
        return false;
    }

    let met: usize = unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_rounds_met_address())
    };
    let refused: usize = unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_refused_address())
    };
    let admitted: usize = unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_admitted_address())
    };
    let floor_bad: usize = unsafe {
        atomic_load_acquire(pool_walk_floor_bad_address())
    };
    if (met != POOL_WALK_ROUNDS) {
        kernel_boot_log("pool walk: a round ended without the walk holding an address in the chunk that was released, so it proved nothing\n");
        return false;
    }
    if (admitted != 0) {
        kernel_boot_log("pool walk: a walk was handed a live payload for a chunk that had been unlinked, scrubbed and given back, which is the defect\n");
        return false;
    }
    if (refused != POOL_WALK_ROUNDS) {
        kernel_boot_log("pool walk: a released chunk's address was neither refused nor admitted, so the probe did not answer\n");
        return false;
    }
    if (floor_bad != 0) {
        kernel_boot_log("pool walk: a floor slot read back something other than the value written to it at setup\n");
        return false;
    }
    // Every page back: each round allocated and freed one chunk, and a
    // walk holding a stale address must not have kept one alive.
    return page_allocator_live_count() == live_before;
}
